## Linux DRM - Architecture and the Big Picture
```require
- Kernel: Based on Linux v6.19 source
- Library: libdrm 2.4.129
```
### Introduction
Drawing anything on the screen in Linux goes through a kernel subsystem called <mark>DRM (Direct Rendering Manager)</mark>. Desktop Wayland or Xorg, Android's SurfaceFlinger, and even the Raspberry Pi console display all operate on top of the same DRM.  

This has nothing to do with <b>Digital Rights Management (copyright protection)</b>, which shares a similar name; this article deals exclusively with the Linux kernel's graphics subsystem.  

In this post, we explore <b>what DRM is and how it connects to the kernel</b>. We will delve deeply into the actual code starting in the next post.  


```adcard
```

### Why DRM Was Needed
Before DRM existed, there were two primary approaches:  

- <b>fbdev (`/dev/fb0`)</b>: A straightforward method that `mmap`s a single chunk of display memory to directly write pixels. It lacks features such as 3D acceleration, multi-monitor support, and resolution changes.
- <b>Direct hardware control by the X server</b>: The X server ran with root privileges to directly access graphics card registers, configuring resolutions (modes) and sending 3D commands.

The second approach had several critical problems:  

- For 3D applications to use the GPU directly, the <b>kernel needed to arbitrate GPU access</b>, but no such mechanism existed (concurrent GPU access by multiple processes caused mutual corruption).
- Because resolution configuration (<b>mode setting</b>) lived in user space, switching between console and X caused screen flickering, and controlling early boot splash screens was impossible.
- The requirement for the X server to run as root posed severe security vulnerabilities.

DRM solved these issues by establishing a model where <b>"the kernel manages the GPU and display, while user space accesses them strictly through safe interfaces."</b> The major milestones were roughly as follows: 

| Feature | Role | Approximate Era |
|---|---|---|
| DRI / DRM | Applications send commands directly to the GPU under kernel mediation | Late 1990s ~ around 2000 |
| GEM | Kernel manages GPU buffers (memory) | Around Linux 2.6.28 |
| KMS | Resolution/display configuration moved into kernel | Around Linux 2.6.29 |
| dma-buf (PRIME) | Shares buffers across processes and devices | Around Linux 3.3 |
| render node | Dedicated nodes for GPU compute without display control | Around Linux 3.12 |
| atomic modeset | Atomically verifies and applies display configuration changes | Linux 3.19 (Kernel doc `Documentation/gpu/todo.rst`) |

The structures covered in this article are the results of this history. Keep in mind that a single subsystem handles both <mark>rendering (drawing with GPU)</mark> and <mark>display (outputting to screen)</mark>.  

### The Full Stack
```mermaid
{
    "title": "Placement of DRM in the Linux Graphics Stack"
}
---
flowchart TB
    subgraph US["User space"]
        APP["3D Apps / Games<br/>OpenGL · Vulkan"]
        COMP["Display Server<br/>Wayland compositor · Xorg"]
        MESA["Mesa<br/>user-mode GPU driver"]
        LIBDRM["libdrm"]
    end
    subgraph KS["Kernel space"]
        NR["/dev/dri/renderD128<br/>render node"]
        NC["/dev/dri/card0<br/>primary node"]
        CORE["DRM core<br/>drm_drv.c · drm_ioctl.c · drm_gem.c ..."]
        HELP["DRM helpers<br/>atomic · gem_shmem · gpu scheduler"]
        DRV["GPU / display driver<br/>i915 · amdgpu · v3d · vkms ..."]
    end
    HW["GPU + display controller + monitor"]
    APP --> MESA --> LIBDRM
    COMP --> LIBDRM
    LIBDRM -->|"ioctl · mmap"| NR
    LIBDRM -->|"ioctl · read"| NC
    NR --> CORE
    NC --> CORE
    CORE --> HELP --> DRV
    CORE --> DRV
    DRV --> HW
```
- <b>Applications</b> draw using OpenGL/Vulkan. <b>Mesa</b>, which implements these APIs, generates GPU-specific commands (shaders, state setup). This part is called the <b>user-mode driver (UMD)</b>.
- <b>libdrm</b> is a thin C library wrapping `ioctl()`. In other words, user space communicates with DRM solely by opening <mark>/dev/dri/*</mark> files and issuing `ioctl`, `mmap`, and `read`.
- <b>The kernel side</b> is divided into the common <b>DRM core</b>, <b>helpers</b> shared across various drivers, and hardware-specific <b>drivers</b>.

Two pathways are visible in the diagram. 3D applications use the <b>render node</b> solely for GPU compute, while display configuration is handled by the <b>display server (compositor) through the primary node (card0)</b>. This separation is at the heart of DRM's design.  

### Two Jobs of DRM
| | Rendering | Display |
|---|---|---|
| Objective | Generates images using the GPU | Outputs generated images to the screen |
| Kernel Features | Memory management (GEM), sharing (dma-buf), synchronization (fence), job submission (scheduler) | KMS (framebuffer, plane, CRTC, encoder, connector) |
| Accessible Nodes | Available via render node (`renderD128`) | Available exclusively via primary node (`card0`) |


```adcard
```

### Device Nodes: /dev/dri and sysfs
First, let's verify this on an actual PC (author's PC: kernel 7.0.0, Intel i915 driver).  

```shell
{
    "title": "Verifying DRM Device Nodes"
}
---
$ grep -w drm /proc/devices
226 drm

$ ls -l /dev/dri/
crw-rw----+ 1 root video  226,   0 card0
crw-rw----+ 1 root render 226, 128 renderD128

$ cat /sys/class/drm/card0/dev /sys/class/drm/renderD128/dev
226:0
226:128

$ readlink -f /sys/class/drm/card0
/sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:00:02.0/drm/card0

$ grep DRIVER /sys/class/drm/card0/device/uevent
DRIVER=i915

$ ls /sys/class/drm/
card0  card0-DP-1  card0-HDMI-A-1  card0-HDMI-A-2  renderD128  version
```
- The major number is <mark>226</mark> and the name is `drm`. This value is registered by the kernel during boot (verified in the code below).
- <b>card0</b> has minor number 0, while <b>renderD128</b> has 128. This indicates that minor number ranges vary depending on the node type.
- <b>Connectors</b> also appear in sysfs, such as `card0-DP-1` and `card0-HDMI-A-1`.

This rule resides in the kernel's `drm_drv.c`.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c - #define DRM_MINOR_LIMIT (L136-139)"
}
---
#define DRM_MINOR_LIMIT(t) ({ \
	typeof(t) _t = (t); \
	_t == DRM_MINOR_ACCEL ? XA_LIMIT(0, ACCEL_MAX_MINORS) : XA_LIMIT(64 * _t, 64 * _t + 63); \
})
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c#L136-L139" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L136-L139</a> (Linux v6.19)

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_file.h - enum drm_minor_type (L61-66)"
}
---
enum drm_minor_type {
	DRM_MINOR_PRIMARY = 0,
	DRM_MINOR_CONTROL = 1,
	DRM_MINOR_RENDER = 2,
	DRM_MINOR_ACCEL = 32,
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_file.h#L61-L66" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_file.h L61-L66</a> (Linux v6.19)

- <b>Primary</b> (`DRM_MINOR_PRIMARY = 0`) spans minor numbers 0–63, while <b>render</b> (`DRM_MINOR_RENDER = 2`) begins at `64 * 2 = 128`. The `226, 128` in the `ls` output above reflects this exact calculation.
- The name is assigned in `drm_sysfs_minor_alloc()`.

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysfs.c - drm_sysfs_minor_alloc() (L551-591)"
}
---
struct device *drm_sysfs_minor_alloc(struct drm_minor *minor)
{
	const char *minor_str;
	struct device *kdev;
	int r;

	kdev = kzalloc(sizeof(*kdev), GFP_KERNEL);
	if (!kdev)
		return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);

	device_initialize(kdev);

	if (minor->type == DRM_MINOR_ACCEL) {
		minor_str = "accel%d";
		accel_set_device_instance_params(kdev, minor->index);
	} else {
		if (minor->type == DRM_MINOR_RENDER)
			minor_str = "renderD%d";  // [!code highlight]
		else
			minor_str = "card%d";  // [!code highlight]

		kdev->devt = MKDEV(DRM_MAJOR, minor->index);  // [!code highlight]
		kdev->class = drm_class;
		kdev->groups = card_dev_groups;
		kdev->type = &drm_sysfs_device_minor;
	}

	kdev->parent = minor->dev->dev;
	kdev->release = drm_sysfs_release;
	dev_set_drvdata(kdev, minor);

	r = dev_set_name(kdev, minor_str, minor->index);
	if (r < 0)
		goto err_free;

	return kdev;

err_free:
	put_device(kdev);
	return ERR_PTR(r);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysfs.c#L551-L591" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysfs.c L551-L591</a> (Linux v6.19)

You can see the names `"renderD%d"`, `"card%d"`, and `MKDEV(DRM_MAJOR, minor->index)`. Once this `struct device` is later registered via `device_add()`, udev and devtmpfs create `/dev/dri/card0` and `/dev/dri/renderD128`.  

#### How card0 Differs from renderD128
The <mark>primary node (card0)</mark> has access to all capabilities (including KMS) and can hold <b>DRM master</b>, the privilege required for display control. The <mark>render node (renderD128)</mark>, while unable to configure displays, is free from permission constraints. Anyone with file access permissions can open it to run GPU computations.  

This distinction is baked into each ioctl via <b>flags</b> and enforced by `drm_ioctl_permit()`.  

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_ioctl.h - enum drm_ioctl_flags (L80-122)"
}
---
enum drm_ioctl_flags {
	DRM_AUTH		= BIT(0),
	DRM_MASTER		= BIT(1),
	DRM_ROOT_ONLY		= BIT(2),
	DRM_RENDER_ALLOW	= BIT(5),
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_ioctl.h#L80-L122" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_ioctl.h L80-L122</a> (Linux v6.19)

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c - drm_ioctl_permit() (L599-621)"
}
---
static int drm_ioctl_permit(u32 flags, struct drm_file *file_priv)
{
	if (unlikely((flags & DRM_ROOT_ONLY) && !capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN)))
		return -EACCES;

	if (unlikely((flags & DRM_AUTH) && !drm_is_render_client(file_priv) &&  // [!code highlight]
		     !file_priv->authenticated))
		return -EACCES;

	if (unlikely((flags & DRM_MASTER) &&  // [!code highlight]
		     !drm_is_current_master(file_priv)))
		return -EACCES;

	if (unlikely(!(flags & DRM_RENDER_ALLOW) &&  // [!code highlight]
		     drm_is_render_client(file_priv)))
		return -EACCES;

	return 0;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c#L599-L621" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L599-L621</a> (Linux v6.19)

Reading through the code makes the rules clear:  

- An ioctl flagged with `DRM_MASTER` can only be invoked by the current master. A prime example is <mark>DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ATOMIC</mark>.
- Render nodes (`drm_is_render_client`) can only execute ioctls where `DRM_RENDER_ALLOW` is <b>explicitly designated</b>. Hence, while `DRM_IOCTL_VERSION` and `DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_HANDLE_TO_FD` work on render nodes, `DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB` has flags set to 0 and is rejected on render nodes.

Then who becomes the master? The first client to open the primary file automatically acquires master status.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_auth.c - drm_master_open() (L317-335)"
}
---
int drm_master_open(struct drm_file *file_priv)
{
	struct drm_device *dev = file_priv->minor->dev;
	int ret = 0;

	guard(mutex)(&dev->master_mutex);
	if (!dev->master) {
		ret = drm_new_set_master(dev, file_priv);
	} else {
		spin_lock(&file_priv->master_lookup_lock);
		file_priv->master = drm_master_get(dev->master);
		spin_unlock(&file_priv->master_lookup_lock);
	}

	return ret;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_auth.c#L317-L335" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_auth.c L317-L335</a> (Linux v6.19)

Therefore, if the desktop environment has already opened `card0` and holds master privileges, any other application attempting display configuration (atomic commit) on that same `card0` will receive `-EACCES`.  


