```

### Struct Relationships Inside DRM Core
There are three core DRM structures: <b>`drm_driver`</b> (a constant template defined by the driver), <b>`drm_device`</b> (representing a single device), and <b>`drm_file`</b> (representing an individual `open()`).  

```mermaid
{
    "title": "Relationships between drm_driver · drm_device · drm_minor · drm_file"
}
---
flowchart TB
    DRIVER["struct drm_driver<br/>const template defined by driver<br/>name · driver_features · fops · ioctls · dumb_create ..."]
    DEV["struct drm_device<br/>instance of 1 physical device<br/>filelist · mode_config · vblank ..."]
    PRI["drm_minor : primary<br/>card0 · 226:0"]
    REN["drm_minor : render<br/>renderD128 · 226:128"]
    F1["drm_file<br/>Process A's open()"]
    F2["drm_file<br/>Process B's open()"]
    PF["Per-open state<br/>object_idr : GEM handle table · fbs : created framebuffers<br/>event_list : event queue · master · atomic settings"]
    DRIVER -->|"dev->driver"| DEV
    DEV --> PRI
    DEV --> REN
    PRI --> F1
    REN --> F2
    F1 --- PF
```
#### drm_driver: The Template Drivers Fill In
A driver defines a single `struct drm_driver` as `const`. Inside are feature flags, file operations, a driver-specific ioctl table, and several callbacks.  

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_drv.h - struct drm_driver (L181-434)"
}
---
struct drm_driver {
	int (*load) (struct drm_device *, unsigned long flags);
	int (*open) (struct drm_device *, struct drm_file *);
	void (*postclose) (struct drm_device *, struct drm_file *);
	void (*unload) (struct drm_device *);
	void (*release) (struct drm_device *);
	void (*master_set)(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file_priv,
			   bool from_open);
	void (*master_drop)(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file_priv);
	void (*debugfs_init)(struct drm_minor *minor);
	struct drm_gem_object *(*gem_create_object)(struct drm_device *dev,
						    size_t size);
	int (*prime_handle_to_fd)(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file_priv,
				uint32_t handle, uint32_t flags, int *prime_fd);
	int (*prime_fd_to_handle)(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file_priv,
				int prime_fd, uint32_t *handle);
	struct drm_gem_object * (*gem_prime_import)(struct drm_device *dev,
				struct dma_buf *dma_buf);
	struct drm_gem_object *(*gem_prime_import_sg_table)(
				struct drm_device *dev,
				struct dma_buf_attachment *attach,
				struct sg_table *sgt);
	int (*dumb_create)(struct drm_file *file_priv,
			   struct drm_device *dev,
			   struct drm_mode_create_dumb *args);
	int (*dumb_map_offset)(struct drm_file *file_priv,
			       struct drm_device *dev, uint32_t handle,
			       uint64_t *offset);
	int (*fbdev_probe)(struct drm_fb_helper *fbdev_helper,
			   struct drm_fb_helper_surface_size *sizes);
	void (*show_fdinfo)(struct drm_printer *p, struct drm_file *f);
	int major;
	int minor;
	int patchlevel;
	char *name;
	char *desc;
	u32 driver_features;
	const struct drm_ioctl_desc *ioctls;
	int num_ioctls;
	const struct file_operations *fops;
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_drv.h#L181-L434" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_drv.h L181-L434</a> (Linux v6.19)

- `driver_features`: Bit flags such as <mark>DRIVER_GEM</mark>, <mark>DRIVER_MODESET</mark>, <mark>DRIVER_RENDER</mark>, and <mark>DRIVER_ATOMIC</mark>. The DRM core inspects this value to decide which features to activate (for instance, `DRIVER_RENDER` must be present to instantiate a render node).
- `fops`: The `file_operations` for `/dev/dri/cardN`. Most drivers use a standard macro for this (seen below).
- `ioctls`, `num_ioctls`: Driver-specific ioctl table. The number range is `0x40` through `0xA0` (`DRM_COMMAND_BASE` to `DRM_COMMAND_END`).
- `dumb_create`, `gem_prime_import_sg_table`, etc.: <b>Driver callbacks</b> invoked by the core. The core handles common tasks and delegates hardware-dependent behavior to these callbacks. This pattern permeates all of DRM.

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_drv.h - enum drm_driver_feature (L57-170)"
}
---
enum drm_driver_feature {
	DRIVER_GEM			= BIT(0),
	DRIVER_MODESET			= BIT(1),
	DRIVER_RENDER			= BIT(3),
	DRIVER_ATOMIC			= BIT(4),
	DRIVER_SYNCOBJ                  = BIT(5),
	DRIVER_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE         = BIT(6),
	DRIVER_COMPUTE_ACCEL            = BIT(7),
	DRIVER_GEM_GPUVA		= BIT(8),
	DRIVER_CURSOR_HOTSPOT           = BIT(9),
	DRIVER_USE_AGP			= BIT(25),
	DRIVER_LEGACY			= BIT(26),
	DRIVER_PCI_DMA			= BIT(27),
	DRIVER_SG			= BIT(28),
	DRIVER_HAVE_DMA			= BIT(29),
	DRIVER_HAVE_IRQ			= BIT(30),
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_drv.h#L57-L170" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_drv.h L57-L170</a> (Linux v6.19)

#### drm_file: One per open()
Each `open()` of `/dev/dri/card0` spawns a `struct drm_file`. All per-process (or per-fd) states reside here.  

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_file.h - struct drm_file (L164-420)"
}
---
struct drm_file {
	bool authenticated;
	bool stereo_allowed;
	bool universal_planes;  // [!code highlight]
	bool atomic;  // [!code highlight]
	bool aspect_ratio_allowed;
	bool writeback_connectors;
	bool plane_color_pipeline;
	bool was_master;  // [!code highlight]
	bool is_master;  // [!code highlight]
	bool supports_virtualized_cursor_plane;
	struct drm_master *master;  // [!code highlight]
	spinlock_t master_lookup_lock;  // [!code highlight]
	struct pid __rcu *pid;
	u64 client_id;
	drm_magic_t magic;
	struct list_head lhead;
	struct drm_minor *minor;
	struct idr object_idr;  // [!code highlight]
	spinlock_t table_lock;
	struct idr syncobj_idr;
	spinlock_t syncobj_table_lock;
	struct file *filp;
	void *driver_priv;
	struct list_head fbs;  // [!code highlight]
	struct mutex fbs_lock;  // [!code highlight]
	struct list_head blobs;
	wait_queue_head_t event_wait;
	struct list_head pending_event_list;  // [!code highlight]
	struct list_head event_list;  // [!code highlight]
	int event_space;
	struct mutex event_read_lock;
	struct drm_prime_file_private prime;  // [!code highlight]
	const char *client_name;
	struct mutex client_name_lock;
	struct dentry *debugfs_client;
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_file.h#L164-L420" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_file.h L164-L420</a> (Linux v6.19)

- <mark>object_idr</mark>: A table mapping user-visible <b>GEM handles</b> to kernel objects. This implies handles are <b>isolated per fd (drm_file)</b>.
- <mark>event_list</mark>, `pending_event_list`: Queues buffering events such as page flip completion. User space retrieves them by calling `read()` on this fd.
- <mark>atomic</mark>, `universal_planes`: Client capabilities enabled via `drmSetClientCap()`. Atomic ioctls require these to be turned on.
- `master`, `is_master`: Indicate whether this file holds display control authority.


