### Source Tree Map
When exploring DRM code, the first question to answer is "what is located where."  

| Location | Contents |
|---|---|
| `include/uapi/drm/` | ABI shared between user space and kernel (`drm.h`, `drm_mode.h`, driver-specific `*_drm.h`) |
| `include/drm/` | Kernel-internal headers (`drm_device.h`, `drm_drv.h`, `drm_gem.h` ...) |
| `drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c`, `drm_file.c`, `drm_ioctl.c` | Core backbone including devices, files, ioctl |
| `drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem*.c`, `drm_prime.c`, `drm_syncobj.c` | Buffer management, sharing, and synchronization |
| `drivers/gpu/drm/drm_atomic*.c`, `drm_crtc.c`, `drm_plane.c`, `drm_vblank.c` | KMS and atomic modesetting |
| `drivers/gpu/drm/scheduler/` | GPU job scheduler |
| `drivers/dma-buf/` | dma-buf, dma-fence |
| `drivers/gpu/drm/<driver>/` | Individual drivers like `i915`, `amdgpu`, `v3d`, `vkms` |
| `Documentation/gpu/` | Official documentation (`drm-kms.rst`, `drm-mm.rst`, `drm-uapi.rst`, `drm-internals.rst`) |

In this series, we will frequently refer to <b>vkms</b> (Virtual Kernel Mode Setting) when illustrating driver structures. Being a virtual driver devoid of hardware dependencies, its codebase is compact (`drivers/gpu/drm/vkms/`, roughly 6,000 lines) and cleanly reveals the minimal skeleton required by DRM.

### Code Locations
| Stage | Function / Struct | Location (Linux v6.19) |
|---|---|---|
| Struct: Driver template | `struct drm_driver` | <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_drv.h#L181-L434" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_drv.h:181</a> |
| Struct: Single open() state | `struct drm_file` | <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_file.h#L164-L420" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_file.h:164</a> |
### Summary
Here is what we covered in this post:  

- DRM is a subsystem through which the kernel arbitrates <b>rendering (GPU)</b> and <b>display (KMS)</b>; user space interfaces with it solely via <b>`ioctl`, `mmap`, and `read`</b> on `/dev/dri/*`.
- Device nodes use <b>major number 226</b> and include <b>primary (card)</b> and <b>render (renderD)</b> nodes, with permission distinctions enforced through ioctl flags (`DRM_MASTER`, `DRM_RENDER_ALLOW`).
- The hierarchy is structured as <b>`drm_driver` (template) → `drm_device` (device) → `drm_minor` (node) → `drm_file` (open)</b>, with the core handling common tasks and delegating hardware-specific routines to <b>driver callbacks</b>.

In the next post, we will walk through the code of the vkms driver to trace the process by which this architecture is materialized: <b>core initialization (registering 226) and driver registration (`drm_dev_register`)</b>.
